---
import Base from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Commands"
  description="Every postiee command with its flags, plus the environment variables that override defaults."
>
  <p class="eyebrow">Reference</p>
  <h1>Commands</h1>

  <p class="lead">
    Six commands do the real work. Everything else exists because the generator
    emitted it, and you will never need it.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Command</th>
          <th scope="col">What it does</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>post</code></td><td>Create a post and record it locally.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>history</code></td><td>Every post recorded locally, newest first.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>search</code></td><td>Full-text search over your own post bodies.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>forget</code></td><td>Remove a post from your local history.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>auth</code></td><td>Login, logout, status.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>doctor</code></td><td>Config, credential, and connectivity check.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>userinfo</code></td><td>Print your app-scoped member ID. See the note below.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <p>
      <strong>You do not need <code>images</code>.</strong> It exists, but
      <code>post --image</code> already uploads and attaches in one step, and
      <code>images</code> on its own wants raw headers spelled out. Same for
      <code>userinfo</code>: it works, but it prints an identifier you never
      type yourself.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>post</h2>

  <CodeBlock
    code={`postiee post "some text"
postiee post --file post.md
postiee post "with a picture" --image chart.png --alt "a line chart"
postiee post "connections only" -v CONNECTIONS`}
  />

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Flag</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>--file</code></td><td>Read the body from a file. Avoids shell quoting entirely.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--image</code></td><td>Attach one image. JPG, PNG, or GIF. One, not many — see the note below.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--alt</code></td><td>Alt text for that image.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>-v</code>, <code>--visibility</code></td><td><code>PUBLIC</code>, <code>CONNECTIONS</code>, or <code>LOGGED_IN</code>. Defaults to <code>PUBLIC</code>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <p>
      Prefer <code>--file</code> for anything long. Quoting a multi-line post
      through a shell is how you end up with mangled apostrophes.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="note warn">
    <p>
      <strong>One image only.</strong> <code>--image</code> takes a single file,
      and that is the whole of what a self-serve app can attach. Multi-image
      posts and video both need LinkedIn partner approval, so there is no flag
      for them. See <a href="/postiee/docs/limits">Limits and scope</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>history</h2>

  <CodeBlock code={`postiee history
postiee history --limit 200`} />

  <p>
    Reads only the local database. No network call, no rate-limit cost.
    <code>--limit</code> defaults to 50.
  </p>

  <h2>search</h2>

  <CodeBlock
    code={`postiee search harness
postiee search "harness agent" --json`}
  />

  <p>
    Terms are ANDed, so more words narrow the result. Porter stemming is on —
    <code>authentic</code> finds <code>authentication</code>.
    <code>--limit</code> defaults to 25.
  </p>

  <div class="note warn">
    <p>
      <strong>Whole words only.</strong> SQLite FTS5 indexes complete tokens, so
      <code>postiee search harn</code> will not find <code>harness</code>. Type
      the full word. This is documented rather than worked around, because a
      search that quietly returns nothing is worse than one that states where it
      stops.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    Search covers posts made through postiee on this machine. Anything you
    posted from LinkedIn's app or website is invisible to every API, including
    this one.
  </p>

  <h2>forget</h2>

  <CodeBlock code="postiee forget urn:li:ugcPost:12345" />

  <p>
    Removes the post from your local history and from the search index. Take the
    URN from <code>history</code>.
  </p>

  <div class="note warn">
    <p>
      <strong>This is local only.</strong> <code>forget</code> deletes your
      record, not the post. Nothing is removed from LinkedIn — no API deletes a
      member's own post at any tier. To take a post down, delete it in LinkedIn
      by hand.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>auth and doctor</h2>

  <CodeBlock
    code={`postiee auth login --port 8085
postiee auth status
postiee auth logout
postiee doctor`}
  />

  <p>
    <code>doctor</code> is the one to run first when something looks wrong. It
    reports where it found your config and credentials, whether a token is
    loaded, and whether <code>api.linkedin.com</code> answers.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <p>
      <strong>Tokens last 60 days.</strong> Automatic refresh is partner-only,
      so an expired token means one more <code>auth login</code>. If you are
      still signed in to LinkedIn the consent screen is skipped and it takes
      seconds. The login window stays open 15 minutes, which covers a password,
      a 2FA prompt, and an emailed code.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Agent mode</h2>

  <CodeBlock code="postiee --agent search harness" />

  <p>
    Sets <code>--json --compact --no-input --no-color</code> together. Use it
    whenever anything is parsing the output.
  </p>

  <h2>Environment variables</h2>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Variable</th><th scope="col">Effect</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>POSTIEE_SCOPES</code></td>
          <td>Override the requested OAuth scopes. Useful during setup if Sign In with LinkedIn isn't added yet.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>POSTIEE_REDIRECT_URI</code></td>
          <td>Override the redirect host spelling. Must match your app's registration exactly.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>POSTIEE_TOKEN</code></td>
          <td>Use a specific access token instead of the stored one.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>POSTIEE_HISTORY_DB</code></td>
          <td>Point the history database somewhere else.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>POSTIEE_CONFIG</code></td>
          <td>Use a specific config file instead of the default location.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</h2>

  <p>
    Most of these fail the same way: LinkedIn answers <code>401</code> or
    <code>403</code> with a short reason, and postiee passes it through. Match
    the first line of the error here.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">What you see</th>
          <th scope="col">Why</th>
          <th scope="col">What to do</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>unauthorized_scope_error</code></td>
          <td>
            Your app wasn't granted a scope postiee asked for. Share on LinkedIn
            gives <code>w_member_social</code>; Sign In with LinkedIn gives
            <code>openid</code> and <code>profile</code>. Both are needed.
          </td>
          <td>
            Open <strong>Products</strong> on your developer app and add whichever
            is missing. If you're mid-setup and just want to post text, set
            <code>POSTIEE_SCOPES=w_member_social</code> to skip the rest.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>redirect_uri does not match the registered value</code></td>
          <td>
            LinkedIn compares redirect URLs as exact strings — host, port, path,
            trailing slash. <code>localhost</code> and <code>127.0.0.1</code> are
            different values to it, and postiee sends
            <code>127.0.0.1</code> by default.
          </td>
          <td>
            Copy the exact string from your app's <strong>Auth</strong> tab into
            <code>POSTIEE_REDIRECT_URI</code>, or re-register
            <code>http://127.0.0.1:8085/callback</code>. <code>auth login</code>
            prints the URI it is sending, so you can compare them directly.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>403</code> from <code>/v2/userinfo</code></td>
          <td>
            The token has no <code>openid</code>. Either the product was just
            added and hasn't propagated, or you logged in before adding it.
          </td>
          <td>
            Add Sign In with LinkedIn, then <code>auth logout</code> and
            <code>auth login</code> again. A fresh token is required; the old one
            won't gain the scope.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>422</code> from <code>/rest/posts</code>, mention of <code>author</code></td>
          <td>
            The member URN couldn't be resolved, which means the same missing
            <code>openid</code>. It is never a bad post body.
          </td>
          <td>Same fix as the row above. Add the product, then re-login.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>state mismatch</code></td>
          <td>
            The callback that arrived carried a different <code>state</code> than
            the one this run generated — a stale login tab, or two logins racing.
          </td>
          <td>
            Run <code>auth login</code> again and use only the URL it prints this
            time. Don't reuse a tab from an earlier attempt.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>no access token found</code></td>
          <td>
            No token on disk, or the one there has expired. Tokens last 60 days
            and can't be refreshed without partner access.
          </td>
          <td>Run <code>auth login</code>. Confirm with <code>auth status</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>401</code> from any endpoint, on a token that worked yesterday</td>
          <td>The 60 days are up.</td>
          <td>Run <code>auth login</code>. Nothing was lost — the local history is a file on your disk, not a LinkedIn-side record.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Search finds nothing for a word you can see in the text</td>
          <td>
            FTS5 matches whole tokens. <code>harn</code> doesn't match
            <code>harness</code>.
          </td>
          <td>Search the full word. Stemming handles endings — <code>authentic</code> finds <code>authentication</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Search finds nothing and the word is complete</td>
          <td>
            The post was made somewhere other than postiee. Only posts postiee
            wrote are in the index.
          </td>
          <td>Nothing to fix. LinkedIn exposes no API for reading posts you made in its own app.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Something fails only in a loop or a script</td>
          <td>
            You've used up the daily budget: 150 requests per member per day. One
            post is one request.
          </td>
          <td>
            Wait until the budget resets. <code>history</code> and
            <code>search</code> are local and never count against it.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</Base>